.SH NAME
ktm-guide-calling-c \- Calling C (chapter 26 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
ktm calls C functions directly: declare one with \fBextern "c"\fR and call it, with no wrapper and no cost beyond a normal call. Most of the C library is already declared in the \fBlibc\fR module. This chapter covers the \fBlibc\fR module, declaring C functions and data, how C types are written in ktm, C structs, passing ktm functions to C as callbacks, linking other C libraries, and how ktm's names stay apart from C's.
.SH The libc module
.PP
\fBlibc\fR is always linked, and \fBimport libc;\fR gives its functions, structs and constants by their C names:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import libc;

i32 main() {
    printf("%.4f %.0f\en", libc.sqrt(2.0), libc.pow(2.0, 10.0));
    printf("%d\en", libc.getenv("PATH") != null);
    printf("%d\en", libc.getpid() > 0);
    printf("%d\en", libc.strlen("hello"));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1.4142 1024
1
1
5
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBprintf\fR itself is C's \fBprintf\fR, declared for every program.
.SS Streams and exit codes
.PP
\fBlibc.stdin\fR, \fBlibc.stdout\fR and \fBlibc.stderr\fR are C's streams, for \fBlibc.fprintf\fR and \fBlibc.fflush\fR\&. \fBprintf\fR writes to \fBlibc.stdout\fR, so the two stay in order:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import libc;

i32 main() {
    printf("checking %d files\en", 2);
    libc.fprintf(libc.stdout, "%s: ok\en", "a.txt");
    libc.fflush(libc.stdout);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
checking 2 files
a.txt: ok
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBlibc.EXIT_SUCCESS\fR and \fBlibc.EXIT_FAILURE\fR are \fBexit\fR\&'s statuses, as in C. \fBlibc.exit\fR ends the program at once, without running \fBdefer\fRs:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import libc;

i32 main() {
    defer printf("not printed: exit skips defers\en");
    printf("leaving\en");
    libc.exit(libc.EXIT_SUCCESS);
    return 1;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
leaving
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBlibc.fprintf\fR\&'s format is checked like \fBprintf\fR\&'s.
.SH Declaring a C function
.PP
For anything \fBlibc\fR doesn't declare, write the prototype yourself, with \fBextern "c"\fR in front:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
extern "c" i32 abs(i32 x);
extern "c" i64 strtol(u8 *s, u8 **end, i32 base);

i32 main() {
    u8 *end;
    printf("%d %d\en", abs(\-5), strtol("ff zz", &end, 16));
    printf("[%s]\en", end);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
5 255
[ zz]
.EE
.RE
.PP
The prototype has to match the C function: ktm takes it at its word, and a wrong one gives wrong results or a crash, not an error.
.PP
A C function that takes a variable number of arguments is declared with a plain \fB\&...\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
extern "c" i32 snprintf(u8 *buf, u64 size, u8 *fmt, ...);

i32 main() {
    u8 buf[32];
    i32 n = snprintf(buf, 32, "%s\-%d", "id", 42);
    printf("%s (%d chars)\en", buf, n);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
id\-42 (5 chars)
.EE
.RE
