.PP
\fBlong\fR is 64 bits on Linux and macOS, the platforms ktm targets. A C string is a \fBu8 *\fR that ends with a zero byte; a ktm \fBu8[]\fR or \fBString\fR passes as one with \fB\&.ptr\fR when it has its terminating zero, as string literals do.
.SH C data
.PP
A C global is declared the same way, by its C name:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
extern "c" u8 **environ;

i32 main() {
    i64 n = 0;
    while (environ[n]) {
        n++;
    }
    printf("%d\en", n > 0);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1
.EE
.RE
.PP
When the symbol differs from the name you want, or between systems, \fB_extern\fR gives the symbol as the assembly spells it, then the declaration. \fBlibc\fR declares \fBstderr\fR this way, because macOS calls it \fB__stderrp\fR (with Mach-O's leading \fB_\fR):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#ifdef IS_MACOS
pub _extern ___stderrp FILE *stderr;
#else
pub _extern stderr FILE *stderr;
#endif
.EE
.RE
.SH C structs
.PP
A C struct is declared with the same fields in the same order; ktm lays structs out as C does (see Structs and unions (see ktm-guide-structs-and-unions(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct tm {
    i32 tm_sec, tm_min, tm_hour, tm_mday, tm_mon, tm_year, tm_wday, tm_yday, tm_isdst;
    i64 tm_gmtoff;
    u8 *tm_zone;
};

extern "c" tm *gmtime_r(i64 *t, tm *out);

i32 main() {
    i64 t = 86400 * 365;
    tm out;
    gmtime_r(&t, &out);
    printf("%d\-%02d\-%02d\en", out.tm_year + 1900, out.tm_mon + 1, out.tm_mday);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1971\-01\-01
.EE
.RE
.PP
A C struct that ktm only passes around by pointer can be declared ahead and never defined (see Structs and unions (see ktm-guide-structs-and-unions(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct FILE;

extern "c" FILE *fopen(u8 *path, u8 *mode);

i32 main() {
    FILE *f = fopen("/nonexistent/file", "r");
    printf("%d\en", f == null);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1
.EE
.RE
.SH Callbacks into ktm
.PP
A ktm function can be passed where C wants a function pointer: ktm functions use the C calling convention (see Function pointers (see ktm-guide-function-pointers(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
extern "c" void qsort(void *base, u64 n, u64 size, fn(void *, void *) i32 cmp);

i32 byValue(i64 *a, i64 *b) {
    if (*a < *b) {
        return \-1;
    }
    return *a > *b;
}

i32 main() {
    i64 nums[4] = {42, 7, 19, 3};
    qsort(nums, 4, sizeof(i64), &byValue);
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        printf("%d ", nums[i]);
    }
    printf("\en");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
3 7 19 42 
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBbyValue\fR takes \fBi64 *\fR where C passes \fBvoid *\fR: a \fBvoid *\fR parameter on one side matches any pointer on the other. To sort in ktm, \fBsort.slice\fR and \fBvec.Vec<T>.sort\fR are simpler (see the library (see ktm-sort(3))).
.SH Other C libraries
.PP
Name the library in the source with \fB#link\fR, or on the command line with \fB\-clibs\fR (see The preprocessor (see ktm-guide-preprocessor(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#link <sqlite3>

extern "c" i32 sqlite3_libversion_number();

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", sqlite3_libversion_number());
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ktm app.ktm \-clibs="\-lcurl \-lm" \-o app
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB#link\fR works for compiled programs, the JIT and the REPL alike; the JIT loads the shared library when the program starts.
.SH Names stay apart
.PP
ktm's own functions and globals get symbols of their own, so a ktm function called \fBread\fR or \fBfree\fR doesn't replace C's for the code that calls C's:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import mem;

void free(u8 *p) {
    printf("the program's own free\en");
}

i32 main() {
    u8 *buf = mem.alloc(64);
    mem.free(buf);
    free(null);
    printf("done\en");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
the program's own free
done
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBmem.free\fR still releases the memory with C's \fBfree\fR: the program's \fBfree\fR is the symbol \fB_K04free\fR, not \fBfree\fR\&. The same holds the other way round in one program: a module's C function is called by the module's name for it, so \fBlibc.strlen\fR is C's \fBstrlen\fR even next to a \fBstrlen\fR of the program's own.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import libc;

u64 strlen(u8 *s) {
    return 1000;
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%d %d\en", strlen("four"), libc.strlen("four"));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1000 4
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
A prototype without \fBextern "c"\fR is a ktm function, which must be defined in the program or in a ktm library; linking fails otherwise.
.IP \(bu 2
In a module, an \fBextern "c"\fR declaration is the module's name (private unless \fBpub\fR) but keeps its C symbol.
.IP \(bu 2
An \fBextern "c"\fR function has one C symbol and can't be overloaded.
.SH Rules
.IP \(bu 2
\fBextern "c" T name(params);\fR declares a C function; \fBextern "c" T name;\fR a C global; \fB_extern SYMBOL declaration\fR binds a declaration to an exact symbol.
.IP \(bu 2
The prototype must match C's; ktm doesn't check it against C.
.IP \(bu 2
C structs are declared with the same fields in the same order.
.IP \(bu 2
ktm functions use C's calling convention and can be C callbacks.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlibc\fR is always linked; other libraries come with \fB#link\fR or \fB\-clibs\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
ktm's names never clash with C's symbols.
